# AI / adaptive sensing (draft)

Full text: [docs/context/04-ai-adaptive-sensing.md](docs/context/04-ai-adaptive-sensing.md).

- Not: values → one ML model → prediction. Multi-stage: quality → features → baseline → cross-modal confidence → event → spatial → explanation.
- Sensor quality ≠ event confidence. Severity ≠ confidence. Environment-event vs sensor-fault vs uncertain (three scores).
- Sensor semantics: PMS and SCD41 are quantitative; BME688 and MQ135 are features, never fake ppm.
- Do not update baseline during confirmed events. Feature windows are configurable.
- V1 deliverables A–H first (quality, baseline, cross-modal C, rule policy, basic anomaly, classifier, basic spatial, explanations). Advanced ML only if it beats simple baselines.
- Information-value-per-energy is a hypothesis. Compare against fixed-rate, battery-only, and threshold policies. Measure energy.
- On-node: deterministic validation, lightweight features, adaptive/power policy. Gateway: heavier ML, multi-node, forecasting. Tiny models on ESP32 are not a V1 claim (Gate C excludes TinyML).
- AI failure → deterministic fallback. Never drop the observation. Never fabricate values or bypass packet validation.
- Persist decision traces in `sampling_decisions`. Hybrid explanations required; SHAP is optional in this draft vs required in SAD — see conflict matrix.
- Production vs experimental models; no silent auto-retrain.
